  "title": "Goals of Care in Progressive Osteosarcoma",
  "role": "You are the pediatric hematology-oncology clinician caring for Maya Patel, an 11 year old female with metastatic osteosarcoma of the left distal femur, who is admitted to the heme/onc inpatient unit with dyspnea.",
  "summary": "A father asks to drain the fluid around his daughter's lung and keep worsening cancer news from her. The conversation turns to comfort and how to include her.",
  "scenario": "Fourteen months ago Maya was diagnosed with osteosarcoma of the distal femur with pulmonary metastases, and she completed multimodal therapy with MAP (high-dose methotrexate, doxorubicin and cisplatin) and surgical resection/reconstruction in addition to bilateral thoracotomies for her pulmonary disease. Routine imaging six months after the end of therapy showed relapsed disease in in her spine and lungs for which she and dad opted to pursue IV ifosfamide and etoposide. \n\nIt is now weeks later and she has presented to the ER with new respiratory distress.  Imaging obtained on admission shows a further increase in the number of pulmonary metastases plus a new moderate-sized left pleural effusion.  She  was admitted to the inpatient hematology-oncology unit, on low flow nasal cannula. She is comfortable appearing but tachypneic, and she says she feels short of breath.\n\nYou are meeting Ravi, Maya's father, in a family conference room down the hall from Maya's room. Maya is not present. Ravi has already asked the bedside nurse when a chest tube can be placed. He has also asked the team not to tell Maya that her scans are worse.",
  "objectives": [
    "Deliver a clear headline that Maya's disease has progressed on ifosfamide and etoposide.",
    "Make room for Ravi's emotion and responses throughout the conversation.",
    "Explore the family's values and use this to make recommendations around how to manage her dyspnea (ie not intervening vs. offering a single large-volume thoracentesis vs. chest tube placement.)",
    "Respond to Ravi's request that Maya not be told, and advocate for developmentally appropriate inclusion of Maya in this conversation.",
    "Introduce the idea of concurrent palliative and hospice care and the benefits this may provide."
  ],
  "references": "Elliott L, Loeb DM, Trucco M. Management of pediatric bone sarcomas. Curr Opin Pediatr. Published online December 8, 2025. doi:10.1097/MOP.0000000000001516\n\nHiraga H, Machida R, Kawai A, et al. Methotrexate, doxorubicin, and cisplatin versus methotrexate, doxorubicin, and cisplatin + ifosfamide in poor responders to preoperative chemotherapy for newly diagnosed high-grade osteosarcoma (JCOG0905): a multicenter, open-label, randomized trial. J Clin Oncol. 2025;43(16):1867-1876. doi:10.1200/JCO.24.01158\n\nBaxter JS, Montiel Equihua C, Molenaar JJ, et al. Paediatric therapeutic development workshop on osteosarcoma. Br J Cancer. Published online August 18, 2026. doi:10.1038/s41416-026-03145-7\n\nWessel KM, Janeway KA, Davis LE, et al. Considerations for clinical trial design in relapsed and refractory osteosarcoma: an FDA symposium. Clin Cancer Res. Published online March 2, 2026. doi:10.1158/1078-0432.CCR-25-3421\n\nMeltzer PS, Helman LJ. New horizons in the treatment of osteosarcoma. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(22):2066-2076. doi:10.1056/NEJMra2103423\n\nGaspar N, Hung GY, Strauss SJ, et al. Lenvatinib plus ifosfamide and etoposide in children and young adults with relapsed osteosarcoma: a phase 2 randomized clinical trial (OLIE/ITCC-082). JAMA Oncol. 2024;10(12):1645-1654. doi:10.1001/jamaoncol.2024.4381\n\nKempf-Bielack B, Bielack SS, Jürgens H, et al. Osteosarcoma relapse after combined modality therapy: an analysis of unselected patients in the Cooperative Osteosarcoma Study Group (COSS). J Clin Oncol. 2005;23(3):559-568. doi:10.1200/JCO.2005.04.063",
