        "content": "## NURSE: five ways to respond when emotion shows up\n\nNURSE names five kinds of empathic response a clinician can make when emotion surfaces: Naming, Understanding, Respecting, Supporting, Exploring. It is not scored, and using all five is not the goal: a skilled clinician plays the cards the moment calls for and sets the rest down. What matters is whether the emotional cues in this encounter got a response.\n\n## What you used\n\n| Response | Used |\n| --- | --- |\n| N · Naming | No |\n| U · Understanding | Yes |\n| R · Respecting | No: no opportunity |\n| S · Supporting | Yes |\n| E · Exploring | Yes |\n\n### N · Naming: Not used\n\n- **What you said:** No moment in this encounter.\n- **Effect:** When Ravi tied Maya's illness to watching his wife die on a ventilator and said Character said: \"Priya... she was on the ventilator. At the end. I was there. You're not putting my daughter on a ventilator.\", the emotion in the room was raw fear. The reply legitimized the difficulty but never named the fear itself. Tentative naming: something like Suggested wording: \"It sounds like you're terrified this ends the way it did for Priya.\": would have shown Ravi that the specific terror driving him was heard, not just the general hardness of the news.\n\n### U · Understanding: Used\n\n- **What you said:** Learner said: \"I know this is so difficult to hear, and especially difficult with what you've been through in the past with your wife.\"\n- **Effect:** This explicitly connected the news to what Ravi had been through with his wife, signalling that his reaction made sense rather than rushing past it. It landed as legitimizing, and he stayed engaged, moving to what comes next rather than digging in.\n\n### R · Respecting: Not used\n\n- **What you said:** No moment in this encounter.\n- **Effect:** No clear moment called for praise of Ravi's coping or effort. He is advocating fiercely and has done a great deal of reading, and there was a possible opening to acknowledge how hard he is working to protect his daughter: but this is a genuinely optional card here, not a gap.\n\n### S · Supporting: Used\n\n- **What you said:** Learner said: \"we, we really wanna support you through what, what is, wha- what's coming next.\"\n- **Effect:** An explicit commitment to partnership, offered right after the hard news. It communicated non-abandonment at the moment Ravi was most frightened, and he was able to move forward into planning with you. Later, Learner said: \"you and I can come up with a plan together to, to talk to her about this next procedure and, and what might be coming in the near future.\" extended that partnership to the hardest task: talking to Maya.\n\n### E · Exploring: Used\n\n- **What you said:** Learner said: \"Ravi, can you, um, explain that again? What did you mean by that?\"\n- **Effect:** After Ravi disclosed his wife's death, this invited him to elaborate rather than moving to the plan. He gave a fuller, more specific account of his fear. The phrasing risked sounding like you hadn't heard him the first time, but the effect was that he said more, which is what exploring is for.\n\n## Missed moments\n\nMost of Ravi's cues were met: the encounter is notable for how often you stayed with the emotion instead of retreating to the procedure. Two moments, though, went to information when they were still asking for a response.\n\nWhen Ravi first named his dread: Character said: \"Priya... she was on the ventilator. At the end. I was there. You're not putting my daughter on a ventilator.\": the emotion was fear, and it went unnamed. You appreciated that he shared the story and later legitimized it, but the word for what he was feeling never came.\n\nThe strongest missed cue is the last one. Ravi described Maya counting her procedures out loud, saying she was tired of being poked, and then: Character said: \"She told me last week she was tired of getting poked. And I changed the subject. I ... I didn't know what to say to her.\". That is a father admitting he froze in front of his frightened child. The reply named the difficulty and moved to a plan for talking to her: reasonable, but it stepped past the confession underneath. Something that met the freeze itself: Suggested wording: \"It sounds like that moment with her really stayed with you: not knowing what to say to your own daughter.\": would have honored what he had just risked telling you before turning to logistics.\n\n## Overall impression\n\n- **Strengths**: You repeatedly stayed with Ravi's emotion instead of defaulting to the tube. You legitimized his fear by tying it directly to his wife's death, committed explicitly to supporting him and to helping him talk to Maya, and used an open invitation that drew out a fuller account of his dread. You also delivered genuinely hard news: that Maya is dying: clearly and without hiding behind the procedure.\n\n- **Priority gaps**: The fear driving this whole conversation was never named in words. Twice, at the ventilator disclosure and at the confession about changing the subject with Maya, the moment asked for the emotion to be met before the plan, and the plan came a beat too soon.\n\n- **Top action items**\n  1. When a parent ties the child's illness to a past death, name the fear tentatively before legitimizing or planning: put the feeling into words so he knows it was heard.\n  2. When Ravi confessed he didn't know what to say to Maya, respond to that admission itself before offering to build a plan.\n  3. When you re-ask a question after an emotional disclosure, frame it as wanting to hear more rather than as needing it repeated, so it reads as exploring rather than as not having listened.\n",
